// Jidaigeki: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== jidaigeki.f01 | feature | three-act
title: The Man Who Drew The Ships
genre: jidaigeki + historical-drama
tone: Still, salt-bright and uneasy
era: 1853 | period
setting: The Uraga coast, at the mouth of Edo Bay | real | village
logline: When four black ships that move without wind anchor off a coast closed to foreigners for two centuries, a low-ranking samurai who can draw is ordered to row out and record exactly what they are, and finds that an accurate picture is a dangerous thing.
premise: The coast guard has spears and a few old cannon. The ships have guns that could reach the capital. Nobody on shore has seen a steam engine. {kenji}, a foot-soldier of the local garrison with a habit of sketching fishing boats, is given a brush and a rowing boat. His lord wants proof the foreigners are not so formidable. What {kenji} sees will not serve.
synopsis: The first act is the arrival: smoke on the horizon, temple bells, families carrying their belongings inland. {kenji} is the kind of samurai the rank has made redundant: he keeps accounts for the garrison and draws when nobody is looking. His superior {captain_okabe} orders drawings for the report to Edo. {kenji} rows out among guard boats and draws what is there: paddle-wheels, a funnel, sixty-four-pounders in ports. An interpreter in the Dutch trade, {arakida}, explains what a boiler is, with a kettle. The second act is what the drawings do. {captain_okabe} looks at the first and has it redone: smaller guns, please, and add a demon's face, for the public prints. Two sets begin to circulate: the monster pictures that sell in Edo by the thousand, and {kenji}'s true ones, which pass hand to hand among the few officials who want to know. A fisherman's widow, {osada}, who mends nets and has more sense than the garrison, asks him which the children should be shown. He is offered promotion for the false set. He is visited at night by a councillor's man about the true one, and told that knowing the range of those guns is either treason or the only patriotism left, depending on who wins the argument in the capital. In the third act the foreigners land to deliver their letter, with a band; the shore is lined with five thousand samurai in armour their grandfathers wore. {kenji} is among them, with a sketchbook up his sleeve. He draws both lines facing each other, accurately, and understands that he is drawing the end of the thing he is. He sends the true set to Edo unsigned. He keeps one sheet: the widow's children on the beach, looking not at the ships but at him drawing.
question: When the truth is unwelcome, is the faithful record loyalty or betrayal?
themes: seeing clearly as a duty; a class on the edge of obsolescence; propaganda and its uses
audience: Adults; lovers of quiet period films
viewpoint: With {kenji}; much of the film is watching a hand draw
stakes: His stipend and neck, the capital's understanding of what it faces, and a coast's fear
conflict: A minor samurai's honest eye against superiors who need the foreigners smaller, or monstrous, but never as they are
ending: He sends the accurate drawings to Edo without his name. The landing happens peacefully. He knows what it means.
experience: Hushed dread, the pleasure of exact observation, and an elegy you see coming like smoke on a horizon
look: Ink line against sea glare; black hulls and white smoke; antique armour on a summer beach
sound: Bells along the coast, oars, a brush on paper. A brass band, distant and absurd.
m: understand | technology | loyalty-vs-truth | bittersweet
@ kenji | Hirai Kenji | lead | protagonist | human | he/him | 31 | To draw what is there | It is the one thing he does well | What is there is not what anyone wants | Mild, exact, braver on paper than in person | From hobbyist to witness to an anonymous patriot | Soft, apologetic, precise about detail | Faded two-sword dress, ink on his sleeve
@ captain_okabe | Captain Okabe Sadanori | supporting | antagonist | human | he/him | 52 | A report that keeps his lord calm | He has a coast he cannot defend | The drawings show it | Proud, worried, a bureaucrat in armour | From ordering lies to wearing his grandfather's helmet on a beach | Barked, formal | Lacquered armour slightly too small
@ arakida | Arakida Eisuke | supporting | ally | human | he/him | 33 | To be understood, by both sides | He is the only man present who speaks their language | Nobody quite trusts a man who can | Quick, ironic, lonely | From explaining a kettle to carrying a letter | Fluent, careful | Plain robes, a Dutch dictionary
@ osada | Osada | co-lead | foil | human | she/her | 36 | To know whether to run | She has three children and a boat she cannot sail alone | Samurai do not tell fishwives things | Sharp, unimpressed, kind | From asking him a question to answering it for him | Coastal dialect, blunt | Net-mender's apron, sun-dark arms
~ kenji - captain_okabe | subordinate and superior | an order to draw | an order to redraw
~ kenji - arakida | artist and interpreter | a diagram of a boiler | the true set carried to Edo
~ kenji - osada | samurai and fisherman's widow | she asks what the children should be shown | he shows them
